{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"unadhesive-salaried-nightheron","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Finding (audit of epic 3405-c0b7)\n**Site:** `error_code_for` branch 6 (`src/rebar/_errors.py:127-130`): `if isinstance(exc, LLMError): return \"llm_unavailable\"`.\n\n**Exception types mis-mapped (the ones whose OWN docstrings forbid the outage framing, or which are self-inflicted stops, NOT provider availability):**\n- `LLMInputRejectedError` — docstring: \"Deliberately NOT an LLMUnavailableError ... must never surface as 'the LLM provider call failed'\". Raised in `llm/run_failure.py:201`.\n- `RunawayToolLoopError` — docstring: \"deliberately NOT an LLMUnavailableError ... must never surface as 'the LLM provider call failed'\". Raised in `llm/runaway_guard.py:100,124`.\n- `LLMBudgetExhaustedError` — \"rebar stopping itself, not a provider failure\". `llm/run_failure.py:96`.\n- `ContextWindowExceededError` — input too big. `llm/pai_retry.py:197,245`.\n- `StructuredOutputError` / `UnretryableOutputError` / `CompletionRecoveryError` — output defects.\n\n**Wrong code:** `llm_unavailable`. Accurate only for `LLMUnavailableError` / `LLMConfigError`.\n\n**Observable vs latent:** LATENT *via error_code_for specifically* — the MCP gate tools intercept these subclasses earlier (Surface 2, ticket for `_structured_llm_failure`) and the gate verdicts degrade in-band (Surface 3), so today no live tool routes these exact types THROUGH `error_code_for`'s branch 6. But it is a reachable code path (any future/other MCP tool body raising one hits the guard) and it is the ROOT of the pattern.\n\n**Proposed fix — READY-TO-FIX (existing vocabulary, no new code, no contended design):** insert an explicit branch BEFORE branch 6 mapping these self-inflicted / input / output subclasses to `command_failed` (the honest lossy catch-all), so they stop falsely claiming an outage; keep genuine `LLMUnavailableError`/`LLMConfigError` → `llm_unavailable`. This deliberately DOES NOT touch the `WorkflowError` family (execute-time `ExpressionError`/parse), which remains the contended design decision in 73d8 / 551b. `command_failed` is strictly less misleading than `llm_unavailable`; a richer per-type vocabulary would be a separate escalate.\n\n**caused_by / class:** origin 8a31 (blanket `LLMError -> llm_unavailable`).","id":"f75f-5509-10c0-4430","parent_id":"3405-c0b7-0436-4b46","priority":2,"tags":["error-taxonomy","ready-to-fix"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"error_code_for branch 6 maps deliberately-non-outage LLMRunnerError subclasses to llm_unavailable"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788056653928170721,"uuid":"66cf98c2-c91f-4cbc-8234-152cf71fa84e"}